WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.750 --> 00:00:13.669 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  when the exhausted straw hats are at the  verge of death  cornered at the port of any slobby by  the countless enormous warships of the  arriving boston call  threatening to obliterate the entire  island at the  very last moment they are saved by none  other  than their ship seeing the pirate boat  arrive  is a heartwarming and tear-jerking scene  that brings the adventure of anna's  lobby to a worthy end  it just feels so cathartic that the ship  has come to protect a crew  and sail with them all one last time  but why how is it that we have built a  relationship  with not a character we love but a ship  it's an easy enough question but after  sitting down to make this video  i had to find out the hard way that it  actually has a really difficult answer  and that answer is anthropomorphism  so once you will have watched this video  and figured out how to pronounce this  word  you will not only be able to use this  technique to improve your own stories  but also have learned a great new word  you can annoy all your friends and  family with  so let's explore how the going mary  seamlessly unites a number of  storytelling devices to create the  perfect adventure story to understand  the entirety of how oda turned a mere  boat into an ingenious story element  we have to look at it from two  perspectives  the going mary as a ship and the going  mary as a character  so what makes the going mary so special  and what are these devices and  techniques that you can use  to take an everyday object and enhance  your entire story with  it to get that emotional reaction from  your audience  i think before we look at how to turn an  object into a character  a great place to start is to understand  the true potential objects in themselves  holds  you see no matter what type of story you  write chances are that they will  not only consist of people characters  wear clothes  swing swords drive cars and eat food  so while all of these things can simply  be part of your story for the sake of  illustration and realism  instead of seeing them as necessary  accessories for your writing  why not make use of these items and make  them part of the experience  to illustrate this point let's return to  one piece because the mary as  a ship actually carries a lot of meaning  and symbolism  that functions as a framework for the  pirate story that oda's writing  first of all the going mary is portrayed  not as a mere vessel for transportation  but as a home for the entire straw hat  crew  the ship is drawn comfortable and  inviting and thus creates a certain  atmosphere in which the crew can relax  socialize  and live together it's a space  associated with positive experiences  and bonding moments for the main  characters  secondly the design of the ship gives  additional characterization to the story  despite being a pirate ship the bright  and friendly appearance of the merry  and most of its rooms immediately tells  us what sort of people live here  this is further enhanced by details like  the smiling lamb figurehead  or nami's tangerine trees that have been  planted on deck  even without knowing anything about the  straw hats  simply looking at their ship alone would  already tell us that these  are good and friendly people and of  course having a jolly roger on its sail  we also keep being reminded that this is  a pirate crew  traveling the seas as a result the ship  and the crew are so closely connected  that when you see the merry  you can't help but think about the straw  hats  thirdly the ship determines the space of  our overall story  by carrying luffy and co from adventure  to adventure  and in the end also closer to the  overall goal  spending time on the ship almost always  tells us that we're about to change  location  and start a new plot thread and in a way  it also functions as a sort of  intermezzo between  arcs where the crew but also the  audience can relax and recover from the  climax of the previous arc  and finally the mary as a ship also  carries a lot  of symbolism in the story or maybe i  should say  it's actually filled to the brim with  symbolism for both crew and reader  alike the pirate flag is a symbol  obviously  for the pirate story we're reading the  tangerine trees are a reminder of nami's  past and home  same as the mary itself is a reminder  for usopp just to name a few  on a larger scale however the ship  symbolizes the spirit of freedom and  adventure  that defines one piece as a story and  the increasing amount of damage it takes  serves as a reminder for all the  adventures the crew has been on  and all the places they have traveled to  so as you see  even when looking at it as nothing more  than a ship the merry already adds  quite a lot to the story and you can be  sure that oda has sunken  a lot of time into its design using an  object to create  atmosphere characterization motion and  symbolism  is a very effective way of drawing your  audience deeper into the story  and make them want to see more of it and  this can be applied to any object you  can think of  like a piece of wood or a ring or even a  chair  to create an immense emotional response  in your audience  but there's even more that you can do to  an object  you can actually make it into a  character itself  i'm definitely not the first one that  calls the going mary a part of the straw  hat crew  and i strongly believe that mary as a  crew member does a wonderful job at  enhancing the characters and the plot of  the story  but how exactly can you give  characterization to a ship  or any object at that this is where the  wonderful term from the beginning of  this video comes into play  anthropomorphism so what exactly is that  anthropomorphism is a literary device  that assigns  human characteristics to non-human  entities like  animals or inanimate objects so now you  might think  well that's great and all but why would  you want to do this  because if done right it introduces  something unusual  and thus captures the reader's interest  and empathy  anthropomorphism is actually quite  popular these days  and of course animated manga are no  exception for this  in the case of the going marry i'm of  course talking about the ship's spirit  that takes on human appearance in the  form of the club outdoor man  as frankie explains to us this is a rare  manifestation of a ship's soul  made possible by the love and care given  to a ship by its crew  thus the small figure that usop  witnesses repairing the broken ship on  skypia  is actually mary herself in other words  the mary being alive  is not a metaphor she can actually  communicate with people  and this is also not exclusive to the  straw hats when mary wants to save her  crew from anna's lobby  she asks iceberg to repair her for one  last journey  this i think is important because while  mary gains human-like characteristics  she still shows mostly traits and  interests associated with the ship  and thus focuses on being a great pirate  ship  repairing herself and carrying its crew  through any hardship so  what then is the consequence of this  technique well the first thing that you  will notice  is that when humanizing an object it can  start forming relationships with your  characters  or heck it can even become a main  character itself  the mary undoubtedly has formed a bond  with all of the straw hats  but luffy and usop in particular  actually communicate with her  usopp promising to repair her and luffy  apologizing  for not taking better care of her  secondly  the going marry offers a completely new  direction for the plot line  she is the cause for stopping at water 7  in the first place  to look for shipwright that might be  able to repair her and thus  triggering the entire water 7 in any  slobby saga  which i shamelessly admit is my favorite  in the entire story  but even more creatively oda actually  uses mary to save the crew from anna's  lobby  this i believe is an especially strong  moment because the straw hats  just declared war against the world  government to save robin  and despite being fought over and  ultimately abandoned  mary had such a strong connection to the  crew that she was compelled to ask  iceberg to fix her so she could save her  crew  one last time and against all odds  having that love and loyalty rewarded by  the ship that came alive through these  emotions in the first place  is just a very powerful moment and so  lastly the mary functions as a link  between all of the straw hats  she was the place they all forged their  relationships on and got to travel  together  and thus we really feel it in the end  when mary gets a viking  funeral and has to say goodbye to her  crew  it's not just feeling sad for a ship  it's feeling sad for a real character  and in the end we even get some  catharsis with the spirit of mary being  able to keep  accompanying the crew on the rest of  their journey  so that's it for today guys if you enjoy  one piece and want to learn more about  storytelling  world building or my thoughts on the  latest one piece chapter  make sure to like and subscribe to  become part of the channel  right now i hope to see you around next  time and  until then stay safe and have a  wonderful week  you

